1. fixed configuration: the closing b for a few member sets (lam=1, eps=1/4, h0=1000)
   D = [0.5]  (N=1)  ->  b = 1020
   D = [0.1, 0.2, 0.3]  (N=3)  ->  b = 1672
   D = [0.01, 0.01, 0.01]...  (N=10)  ->  b = 4.945e+05
   D = [0.001, 0.001, 0.001]...  (N=100)  ->  b = 4.935e+08
   every set closes; b grows like 1/D_min^2, never fails

2. uniform menu: how far N members can block, greedily, from h0 = 1000 with
   b1/h0 = 0.154 (0359's ratio at N = 1, log P = 1). A clean b exists only above
   the last top. In log10 of b:
      N     top_1     top_2     top_3     top_4        top_N
      1       3.8                                       3.81
      2       4.1       6.3                             6.34
      3       4.3       6.9      12.0                     12
      5       4.5       7.5      13.6      25.7         49.9
     15       5.0       9.0      16.9      32.8     6.52e+04
   top_k squares at every step: with N members the menu must reach b ~ h0^(2^N),
   doubly exponential in N. At N = 15 log T + 73 that is beyond any polynomial in T.
   That is the death of a uniform L. A fixed configuration closes at item 1's b.
